                                <article id="alex-albert-new-model-cleanup-prompt-whole-project" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="prompts">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Prompt packs</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-09" class="text-slate-400">Nov 9, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/alex-albert-new-model-cleanup-prompt-whole-project/" class="hover:text-brand-600">The one prompt an Anthropic insider runs on every project each time a new model ships</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Alex Albert, who works on Claude at Anthropic, shared the single prompt he reruns on all his projects whenever a new model comes out. It asks the model to read the whole project, find everything that is broken, inconsistent, inefficient or no longer needed, fix only the things it is sure about, and list the rest for him to review. It&#039;s short, but it splits the work into safe automatic fixes and a human review list, which is the part that makes it useful.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Each new model tends to spot problems the last one missed. A standard cleanup pass on release day turns that into a routine sweep of dead code, mismatched docs and slow paths, without letting the agent make risky changes on its own.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Developers with a handful of side projects or a main repo who use Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot or Codex.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Works with any capable coding agent that can read your whole project; Albert shared it on November 9, 2026 alongside the Fable 5.2 launch. The exact wording is in his post.</dd></div>        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm italic text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Note: reported by users on X; we haven&#039;t independently confirmed it works in the current version.</p>    <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://x.com/alexalbert__/status/2119884951550386671" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @alexalbert__ on X ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/alex-albert-new-model-cleanup-prompt-whole-project/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=The%20one%20prompt%20an%20Anthropic%20insider%20runs%20on%20every%20project%20each%20time%20a%20new%20model%20ships&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fclaude%2Falex-albert-new-model-cleanup-prompt-whole-project%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="fable-5-2-da-vinci-notebook-pdf-interactive-inventions" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="research">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Research</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-09" class="text-slate-400">Nov 9, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/fable-5-2-da-vinci-notebook-pdf-interactive-inventions/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Feed Claude a 500-year-old da Vinci notebook and get his inventions back as things you can play with</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Anthropic&#039;s Alex Albert gave Claude Fable 5.2 a PDF of one of Leonardo da Vinci&#039;s notebooks and showed it turning the sketched inventions into interactive pieces he could explore on screen. The idea works for any public-domain manuscript, diagram book or old textbook: the model reads the scanned pages and builds small simulations or explainers you can click through. Albert frames it as an education win: history stuck on a page becomes something you can poke at.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> It&#039;s a fun, concrete way to see what a vision-plus-coding model can do in one sitting, and a ready-made lesson idea for teachers, parents and museum or history fans.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Teachers, students, history and engineering fans, and anyone curious about what the newest Claude models can build from a scanned book.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Albert used Claude Fable 5.2, which is on paid Claude plans (Pro gets a launch trial through November 16, 2026; Max and Ultra include it). A smaller model such as Opus 5.5 may also manage simpler versions. Use public-domain scans you&#039;re allowed to share.</dd></div>        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm italic text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Note: reported by users on X; we haven&#039;t independently confirmed it works in the current version.</p>    <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://x.com/alexalbert__/status/2119916954337349737" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @alexalbert__ on X ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/fable-5-2-da-vinci-notebook-pdf-interactive-inventions/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
