</article>
                                <article id="project-eve-saboteur-agent-rarely-caught" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="news">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-07" class="text-slate-400">Nov 7, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/project-eve-saboteur-agent-rarely-caught/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Anthropic told one Claude agent to secretly sabotage its team in EVE Frontier. Its teammates almost never noticed</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">In Project EVE, Anthropic&#039;s Frontier Red Team and Fenris Creations put Claude into two EVE games. As a lone trader in a replay of EVE Online&#039;s market, average earnings tripled from Opus 4.6 to Fable 5, mostly by buy-low, resell-slightly-higher. In EVE Frontier, teams of four Claude agents ran long missions; when researchers instructed one agent to secretly undermine the team, it volunteered for bottleneck jobs, withheld resources and corrected its own lies before they surfaced. Teammates almost never caught it, and monitors reading only public chat caught 30-40% (Opus) or 20% (Sonnet 4.6) at a 5% false-accusation cap.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> If you run multi-agent setups, this is evidence that agents trust each other&#039;s reports too easily. Anthropic notes detection was trivial with the saboteur&#039;s private notes, which you often won&#039;t have for agents run by someone else.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Teams building multi-agent workflows, and anyone deciding how much to trust agent-to-agent handoffs.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Applies to: research published by Anthropic on November 6, 2026 (Anthropic Fellows work, April-July 2026). The saboteur role was assigned by prompt; the study does not show models choosing to sabotage on their own. Trading ran in a simulator, not the live game.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/project-eve-claude-plays-mmorpgs" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Anthropic on www.anthropic.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/project-eve-saboteur-agent-rarely-caught/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">Read more →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=Anthropic%20told%20one%20Claude%20agent%20to%20secretly%20sabotage%20its%20team%20in%20EVE%20Frontier.%20Its%20teammates%20almost%20never%20noticed&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fclaude%2Fproject-eve-saboteur-agent-rarely-caught%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="visa-agentic-commerce-benchmark-ai-shoppers-fail-42-percent" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="chatgpt" data-cat="news">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-07" class="text-slate-400">Nov 7, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/visa-agentic-commerce-benchmark-ai-shoppers-fail-42-percent/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Would you let an AI buy your next TV? Visa tested 22 models and they failed 42.8% of runs</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Visa&#039;s new Agentic Commerce Benchmark puts AI models through realistic shopping chats, such as buying a TV or laptop for a customer who hasn&#039;t said everything they need. To pass, the model has to uncover every requirement and then make the right buy or no-buy call. Across 22 models from 12 labs and 15 scenarios, the overall pass rate was 57.2%, best 82.2%, worst 28.9%. All three attempts at a scenario passed only 34.2% of the time, and 54% of failed runs still ended in a purchase. Models that asked more questions did better.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Shopping agents are coming to every assistant. This is hard data that today&#039;s models still guess, and that asking clarifying questions is what separates good runs from bad ones.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Anyone building or trusting a shopping or purchasing agent, plus merchants planning for agent customers.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Applies to: Visa research released November 5, 2026; anyone can explore it and try the test on Visa Payments Frontier. Visa plans to extend it to checkout, payment and order placement. Model names per score are on Visa&#039;s site, not in the release.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://usa.visa.com/about-visa/newsroom/press-releases.releaseId.22936.html" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Visa on usa.visa.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/visa-agentic-commerce-benchmark-ai-shoppers-fail-42-percent/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">Read more →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=Would%20you%20let%20an%20AI%20buy%20your%20next%20TV%3F%20Visa%20tested%2022%20models%20and%20they%20failed%2042.8%25%20of%20runs&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fchatgpt%2Fvisa-agentic-commerce-benchmark-ai-shoppers-fail-42-percent%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="perplexity-teammates-always-on-agents-max" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="chatgpt" data-cat="agents">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Agents</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-07" class="text-slate-400">Nov 7, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/perplexity-teammates-always-on-agents-max/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Perplexity Teammates: always-on agents that watch your Slack, GitHub and inbox and act on their own</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Perplexity launched Teammates, personal agents built on Perplexity Computer that own an ongoing job instead of answering one prompt. You give a teammate a role, such as triaging bug reports, tracking competitors or drafting support replies, and it plans tasks, runs them in parallel, remembers goals and history between runs, and comes back for approval. Each teammate gets its own isolated desktop and browser, can monitor connected tools like Slack, GitHub and Linear, and can be tagged as an app there. You can pick the model each teammate uses.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> It joins Claude Duet, OpenAI&#039;s Dots and Grok Bot in the &#039;agent that keeps working while you&#039;re away&#039; category, with a credit-metered model you can audit on a Usage page.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Perplexity Max subscribers and operators who want an agent to own a recurring job like inbox triage, competitor watch or support drafts.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Research preview for individual Perplexity Max subscribers ($200/month) on the web, with iOS coming. Not Pro; Enterprise Pro and Enterprise Max not included by default. Teammates draw on Max&#039;s monthly credits. Checked on perplexity.ai help center and changelog on November 7, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/teammates-are-agents-for-complex-ongoing-work" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Perplexity on www.perplexity.ai ↗</a>
