                                <article id="brian-armstrong-chief-of-staff-agent-one-queue" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="agents">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Agents</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-07" class="text-slate-400">Nov 7, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/brian-armstrong-chief-of-staff-agent-one-queue/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Coinbase&#039;s CEO now runs a chief-of-staff agent over always-on lead agents, and clears their work from one queue. Here&#039;s how to copy it</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Coinbase CEO Brian Armstrong says he has changed how he works: one lead agent per area of focus runs all the time, and a chief-of-staff agent oversees them. Each lead manages sub-agents that write code, test it, chase reviews and coordinate with teammates. Everything he needs to decide lands in one prioritized queue, a simple local web app where each product review comes with screenshots and a video demo, and he clears it in batches by voice and keyboard shortcuts. The system keeps learning his preferences into what he calls a &quot;Brian brain&quot;, while every change still goes through the same human review and security checks.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> The bottleneck with many agents isn&#039;t the agents, it&#039;s you switching between dozens of chats. A single review queue with demos attached is a pattern anyone running several coding agents can borrow.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Founders, engineering leads and solo builders juggling several Claude Code, Codex or Cursor agents at once.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Armstrong described his own internal setup in a post on X on November 7, 2026 (over 1.5 million views). He didn&#039;t name the models or tools behind it, and it isn&#039;t a product you can buy. The steps below are our suggestion for a smaller version using Claude Code subagents, which come with Claude Pro and higher plans.</dd></div>        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm italic text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Note: this describes one person&#039;s setup as he shared it on X; we haven&#039;t seen the system ourselves.</p>    <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://x.com/brian_armstrong/status/2119112609223827547" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @brian_armstrong on X ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/brian-armstrong-chief-of-staff-agent-one-queue/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=Coinbase%27s%20CEO%20now%20runs%20a%20chief-of-staff%20agent%20over%20always-on%20lead%20agents%2C%20and%20clears%20their%20work%20from%20one%20queue.%20Here%27s%20how%20to%20copy%20it&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fclaude%2Fbrian-armstrong-chief-of-staff-agent-one-queue%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="dots-use-sign-in-codes-from-your-inbox" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="chatgpt" data-cat="agents">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Agents</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-07" class="text-slate-400">Nov 7, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/dots-use-sign-in-codes-from-your-inbox/" class="hover:text-brand-600">ChatGPT dots can now grab the login code from your inbox and sign in for you, if you let them</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">OpenAI&#039;s dots, the always-on agents that keep working in their own cloud browser, used to stall whenever a website emailed a one-time sign-in code. James Sun, who works on browser use for Codex and ChatGPT at OpenAI, says a dot can now use sign-in codes sent to your connected inbox to finish the login for you. When it happens you&#039;re prompted to allow it once or to always let the dot fill codes in, and he says more sign-in improvements are coming.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Email codes were one of the last places an errand-running agent had to hand the job back to you. Fewer stalls means bookings and account chores actually finish while you&#039;re away, but it also widens what the agent can reach.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">ChatGPT Pro, Business Premium and Enterprise users who hand dots multi-step errands on websites that send email codes.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Dots are available to ChatGPT Pro users aged 18+ outside the EEA, Switzerland and the UK, to Business Premium in supported regions, and to eligible Enterprise, Edu and Healthcare workspaces (admin preview until November 18, 2026, then on by default unless the admin opts out). Your personal email must be connected under Customize &gt; Plugins. Announced by OpenAI&#039;s James Sun on X on November 7, 2026; it isn&#039;t listed in OpenAI&#039;s public release notes yet.</dd></div>        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm italic text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Note: announced by an OpenAI engineer on X; it isn&#039;t in OpenAI&#039;s public release notes yet, so it may not have reached every account.</p>    <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://x.com/JamesZmSun/status/2119147164039618879" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @JamesZmSun on X ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/dots-use-sign-in-codes-from-your-inbox/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=ChatGPT%20dots%20can%20now%20grab%20the%20login%20code%20from%20your%20inbox%20and%20sign%20in%20for%20you%2C%20if%20you%20let%20them&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fchatgpt%2Fdots-use-sign-in-codes-from-your-inbox%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="code-email-user-agent-leak-update-now" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="news">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-06" class="text-slate-400">Nov 6, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/code-email-user-agent-leak-update-now/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Claude Code was putting your email in every User-Agent. Update to 2.1.317+ today</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Claude Code sometimes put the signed-in account email into HTTP User-Agent headers when it ran web searches or curl-style tool calls, so third-party sites could see your address without you approving it. Users reported spikes of scam and scam mail after the leak. Anthropic acknowledged the bug on the public GitHub issue: a built-in privacy mod (cc-plugin-privacy) ships on by default in Claude Code 2.1.317 and later, and the team said an upcoming release will stop putting the account email into the model&#039;s context at all. The stable channel was still on 2.1.311 when the issue blew up, so many people still need to update by hand.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> If Claude Code browses for you, sites you never meant to share an email with may already have it. Updating today cuts off the leak before the next research session.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Anyone running Claude Code on Pro, Max, Team or Enterprise, especially people who let it fetch pages or call APIs.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Applies to Claude Code users. Mitigation: update to 2.1.317 or later (releases include 2.1.317 and 2.1.318) and confirm the built-in privacy mod is enabled under /plugins. Anthropic said a fuller fix that removes the account email from model context should arrive early the following week. Checked on GitHub issue #107757 and Anthropic engineer comments on November 6, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/107757" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: Anthropic / Claude Code on github.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/code-email-user-agent-leak-update-now/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">Read more →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=Claude%20Code%20was%20putting%20your%20email%20in%20every%20User-Agent.%20Update%20to%202.1.317%2B%20today&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fclaude%2Fcode-email-user-agent-leak-update-now%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
            <button type="button" data-copy-text="Update Claude Code to 2.1.317 or later, open /plugins, confirm the built-in privacy mod is on, then ask Claude to fetch a public page and watch that the User-Agent it builds does not include your account email." class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200">Copy prompt</button>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="threadrig-free-mac-parallel-agents" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="deals">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Free offers &amp; deals</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-06" class="text-slate-400">Nov 6, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/threadrig-free-mac-parallel-agents/" class="hover:text-brand-600">ThreadRig is a free native Mac app that runs Claude Code, Codex and Gemini in parallel worktrees</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">ThreadRig is a free, native macOS app for Apple silicon that puts Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI side by side in one window. Each thread gets its own Git worktree so agents can edit in parallel without touching your main checkout, and a built-in browser lets them click through local servers to verify changes. You bring your own signed-in CLIs and plans; ThreadRig says it does not create an account for you, does not store AI API keys, and ships without an analytics or crash SDK. Brain lets you assign a default model and effort per kind of work (research, build, execute, design, orchestrate).</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Parallel agents usually mean juggling terminals and fighting merge conflicts. One free native window with isolated worktrees and a browser is a lot less babysitting.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Mac developers on Apple silicon who already pay for Claude Code, Codex or Gemini CLI and want them working on one repo at once.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Who qualifies: anyone with an Apple silicon Mac on macOS 14 or later and at least one supported CLI signed in. Amount: the app is $0; you use your existing provider plans. Deadline: none stated. Afterwards: still free per the site; provider usage bills as usual. Checked on threadrig.com on November 6, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://threadrig.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @ThreadRig on threadrig.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/threadrig-free-mac-parallel-agents/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=ThreadRig%20is%20a%20free%20native%20Mac%20app%20that%20runs%20Claude%20Code%2C%20Codex%20and%20Gemini%20in%20parallel%20worktrees&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fclaude%2Fthreadrig-free-mac-parallel-agents%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
            <button type="button" data-copy-text="On an Apple silicon Mac, download ThreadRig, open a small Git repo, and ask it to run two independent test-fix threads in parallel. Confirm each gets its own worktree and that your main checkout stays clean until you integrate." class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200">Copy prompt</button>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="parallel-mcp-2-free-keyless-web-research" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="deals">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Free offers &amp; deals</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-06" class="text-slate-400">Nov 6, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/parallel-mcp-2-free-keyless-web-research/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Give Claude Code free web search in two minutes: Parallel MCP 2.0 needs no key</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Parallel shipped MCP 2.0 as one hosted server that gives coding agents web_search, web_fetch and web_decisions with no account and no API key. Point Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or VS Code at https://mcp.parallel.ai/mcp and the agent can look up docs, pull page text and ask grounded yes/no or scored questions against the live web. Sign in only when you want the deeper web_research tool, higher rate limits or org controls like domain exclusion. Parallel says anonymous use stays free at lower rate limits, and a signed-in account also gets $5 of free CLI credits each month.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> A coding agent that cannot read the live web guesses outdated APIs. Two minutes of setup stops that without buying another search plan.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Developers on Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or VS Code who want current docs and page text inside the agent without wiring an API key.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Who qualifies: anyone with an MCP-compatible coding agent. Amount: free and keyless for web_search, web_fetch and web_decisions at lower rate limits; optional sign-in for web_research, higher limits and admin controls; Parallel CLI accounts also get $5 free credits per month. Deadline: none stated. Afterwards: still free for anonymous MCP use per Parallel&#039;s post. Checked on parallel.ai/blog/parallel-mcp-2 and the coding-agent setup guide on November 6, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://parallel.ai/blog/parallel-mcp-2" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Parallel on parallel.ai ↗</a>
