                                <article id="github-git-storage-5000-agents-7200-pushes-per-second" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="chatgpt" data-cat="news">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-06" class="text-slate-400">Nov 6, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/github-git-storage-5000-agents-7200-pushes-per-second/" class="hover:text-brand-600">5,000 agents, one repo, 7,200 pushes a second: GitHub rebuilt Git storage for agent swarms</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">GitHub published the details of Project Rubicon, its new Git storage architecture built for repositories that many coding agents push to at once. In internal tests it handled 5,000 concurrent agents on a single repository sending 7,200 pushes per second while reads stayed fast. The trick is that only branch and tag updates need coordination: pack data goes to Azure Blob Storage, a small replicated log service called Ref-CAS orders reference updates, and background compaction nodes keep pack counts flat. GitHub says one repo&#039;s traffic grew about 4x from March to May as agents piled in.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> If you run fleets of agents against one monorepo, push contention and slow fetches become the bottleneck. GitHub is planning for at least 100x more writes per repo, plus clone-free agent access and atomic multi-file commits.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Teams running many parallel coding agents (Copilot, Codex, Claude Code) against the same repository, and platform engineers planning for agent-scale CI.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Applies to GitHub.com repositories. GitHub says the architecture is in limited private preview now, with a broader rollout early next year. Announced at GitHub Universe and detailed on the GitHub Blog on November 5, 2026; checked November 7, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://github.blog/engineering/architecture-optimization/inside-githubs-new-git-storage-architecture/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @GitHub on github.blog ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/github-git-storage-5000-agents-7200-pushes-per-second/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">Read more →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=5%2C000%20agents%2C%20one%20repo%2C%207%2C200%20pushes%20a%20second%3A%20GitHub%20rebuilt%20Git%20storage%20for%20agent%20swarms&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fchatgpt%2Fgithub-git-storage-5000-agents-7200-pushes-per-second%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
</div>
</article>
                                <article id="github-copilot-verified-proficiency-no-exam" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="chatgpt" data-cat="coding">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Coding</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-06" class="text-slate-400">Nov 6, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/github-copilot-verified-proficiency-no-exam/" class="hover:text-brand-600">No exam, just your work: GitHub now scores how well you actually use Copilot, and Proficient earns a badge</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">GitHub Copilot Verified Proficiency gives you a skill level based on how you use Copilot in real work instead of a proctored test. It looks at breadth, depth and consistency across five skills: AI collaboration, prompting, code generation and validation, agent configuration, and tool and context integration. Levels today are Beginner, Intermediate and Proficient, with Advanced and Expert in development. Reaching Proficient earns a Microsoft Pro Badge you can share, valid for six months while you keep that level.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Hiring managers keep asking whether people can really work with AI agents. This gives you a shareable signal from actual usage, and the level page shows which agent skills to practice next.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Developers who use GitHub Copilot daily and want a credential for it, plus engineering leads planning Copilot training.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Opt-in and private by default on learn.github.com; results are visible only to you unless you share them. Based on the last 30 days of Copilot activity and updated daily. Not available for Enterprise Managed User or data-residency accounts yet. GitHub says it is not meant for performance reviews. Announced by GitHub on November 6, 2026; checked on learn.github.com on November 7, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://learn.github.com/proficiencies/copilot/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @GitHub on learn.github.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/github-copilot-verified-proficiency-no-exam/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=No%20exam%2C%20just%20your%20work%3A%20GitHub%20now%20scores%20how%20well%20you%20actually%20use%20Copilot%2C%20and%20Proficient%20earns%20a%20badge&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fchatgpt%2Fgithub-copilot-verified-proficiency-no-exam%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
