    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Coding</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-04" class="text-slate-400">Nov 4, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/ripple-65b-open-weights-apache-32-million/" class="hover:text-brand-600">A 5-person team trained a 65B coding model for $32 million that beats US open models 10x its size, and it&#039;s free to download</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Ricursive Intelligence released Ripple-65B Preview as open weights under the Apache 2.0 license. It&#039;s a 65-billion-parameter dense model trained from scratch on 12 trillion tokens with up to 1 million tokens of context, tuned mainly for agentic coding. Ricursive reports 80.52 on TerminalBench 2.1, ahead of much larger US open-weight models such as Meta&#039;s Muse Spark Open 816B (76.4). The company says the whole project cost $32 million in R&amp;D and that the team grew from 1 to 5 people during development. At 65B it fits on a single NVIDIA DGX Spark, and there&#039;s an NVFP4 checkpoint for local use.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Apache 2.0 means you can use it commercially with almost no strings, and 65B is small enough to run on one high-end workstation, so a capable local coding model is within reach.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Developers who want a commercially usable local coding model, and teams evaluating open models for private codebases.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Free weights on Hugging Face (BF16 and NVFP4 instruct, plus a base model) under Apache 2.0; also hosted by Together AI, GMI Cloud, OpenRouter and Fireworks at their usual per-token prices. It&#039;s a Preview with light post-training, and the benchmarks are Ricursive&#039;s own. Checked on ricursive.com and the Hugging Face model card on November 5, 2026.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://www.ricursive.com/ripple" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: Ricursive Intelligence on www.ricursive.com ↗</a>
