                                <article id="off-by-default-k12-student-accounts" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="gemini" data-cat="news">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/gemini/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-sky-100 text-sky-800 dark:bg-sky-500/15 dark:text-sky-300">Gemini</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-03" class="text-slate-400">Nov 3, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/gemini/off-by-default-k12-student-accounts/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Google will switch Gemini, and even Search, off by default for K-12 student accounts</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Google says Gemini, Gemini Notebook, Gemini in Classroom and Google Search will be turned off by default for K-12 student accounts in Google Workspace for Education, and school admins can switch each one back on separately. Google says schools asked for more control over student AI use, and more details are coming in the months ahead. Search for schools is also changing: AI Mode and AI Overviews will walk students through problems step by step instead of handing over answers, with a pilot in select US schools later this month. Guided Learning is now in Google Classroom worldwide.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> This is the biggest AI vendor in schools choosing opt-in over opt-out for kids. Because Search now has AI built in, it gets switched off too, which is a big change for school IT teams to plan for.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">K-12 Workspace admins, teachers, and parents of students on school Google accounts.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Applies to: K-12 student accounts in Google Workspace for Education. Google hasn&#039;t given an exact date yet and says details are coming in the months ahead. The Search learning pilot starts in select US schools later in November.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/education/ai-learning-era-updates-2026/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Google on blog.google ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/gemini/off-by-default-k12-student-accounts/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">Read more →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
    <a href="https://x.com/intent/post?text=Google%20will%20switch%20Gemini%2C%20and%20even%20Search%2C%20off%20by%20default%20for%20K-12%20student%20accounts&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fagentstacklist.com%2Ftrending%2Fgemini%2Foff-by-default-k12-student-accounts%2F%3Futm_source%3Dx%26utm_medium%3Dsocial%26utm_campaign%3Dshare" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-share-x class="inline-flex min-h-10 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-700 hover:border-brand-400 hover:text-brand-600 dark:border-white/15 dark:text-zinc-200"><svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-3.5 fill-current"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"/></svg>Share on X</a>
    </div>
