                                <article id="openai-bug-bounty-up-to-172k" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="chatgpt" data-cat="news">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-11-02" class="text-slate-400">Nov 2, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/openai-bug-bounty-up-to-172k/" class="hover:text-brand-600">OpenAI now pays up to $172,000 for one bug in ChatGPT or Codex, and a zero-click account takeover just jumped from $6,500 to over $51,000</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">OpenAI raised the rewards in its Security Bug Bounty program on November 2. It says the midpoint of its reward ranges for medium, high and critical findings has more than quadrupled on average. A critical bug in a core product (ChatGPT, Codex or the API Platform) now earns up to $30,000, and exceptional critical findings stay eligible for up to $100,000, or up to $172,000 once new bonuses are added. The bonuses are a quality multiplier for reports with a reliable proof of concept, a five-tier loyalty program and priority campaigns for newly launched features. OpenAI&#039;s own example: a zero-click ChatGPT account takeover that historically paid $6,500 would now pay $51,577 with bonuses.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> It puts a much higher price on finding holes in the AI tools millions of people use, and pending reports get the new rates too. It came alongside OpenAI&#039;s plan for a security sprint with Cathedral, whose researchers will use OpenAI&#039;s models to test its defenses.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Security researchers and bug hunters, and teams that rely on ChatGPT, Codex or the OpenAI API and want to know how seriously their security is treated.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Open to researchers through OpenAI&#039;s Security Bug Bounty program; the full rules and scope are on the program page. Already-submitted reports that haven&#039;t been paid get the new ranges, and bounties paid in the 30 days before November 2 get topped up if they&#039;d have earned more. OpenAI&#039;s security team will also be at PROMPT2PWN in Oakland on November 20-21.</dd></div>        </dl>
