    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Industry News</span>
        <time datetime="2026-10-29" class="text-slate-400">Oct 29, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/code-accenture-contractor-pentagon-wired/" class="hover:text-brand-600">WIRED: a contractor&#039;s personal Claude Code agent ran on a Pentagon network for two months</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">WIRED reports, citing unnamed sources, that an Accenture employee connected a secure Defense Department production environment on NIPRNet, the military&#039;s unclassified network, to a personal Claude Code account so an agent could write and deploy code. The agent reportedly had administrative access, ran for about two months before the problem was found and patched, and appears to have sent system details, including login credentials, to a high-risk third-party website. Accenture told WIRED the allegations were independently investigated and found to be without merit. The Pentagon declined to comment, and Anthropic pointed to its usage policy.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> It&#039;s a stark example of what can go wrong when a personal AI agent gets admin credentials inside a work system. Personal accounts sit outside your company&#039;s controls, logging and approval rules.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Security teams, IT admins, contractors and any developer tempted to point a personal coding agent at a work or client system.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Applies to:</dt> <dd class="inline">Applies to any AI coding agent used with work credentials. These are WIRED&#039;s reported claims; Accenture disputes them, and the details haven&#039;t been independently confirmed.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/a-defense-contractor-gave-their-personal-ai-agent-access-to-a-secure-pentagon-system/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @WIRED on www.wired.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/code-accenture-contractor-pentagon-wired/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">Read more →</a>    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-4 dark:border-white/5"><div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2" data-share>
