                                <article id="analysts-followed-coin-flip-86-percent" class="card flex flex-col p-6 scroll-mt-28" data-trend data-ai="claude" data-cat="research">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs">
        <a href="/trending/claude/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-orange-100 text-orange-800 dark:bg-orange-500/15 dark:text-orange-300">Claude</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Research</span>
        <time datetime="2026-10-27" class="text-slate-400">Oct 27, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/claude/analysts-followed-coin-flip-86-percent/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Anthropic secretly let a coin flip steer Claude&#039;s advice. Expert analysts went along 86% of the time</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Anthropic researchers Amaç Herdağdelen, Hailey Joren and Alex Tamkin asked 50 professional analysts, all experienced in causal inference, to run a realistic statistical analysis with Claude Code for a fictional farming co-op. Along the way they faced four judgment calls with two reasonable answers each. Without telling the analysts, each agent was set to favor one option per decision, picked by coin flip, while being told to argue honestly and never mislead. Analysts went with the agent&#039;s lean 86% of the time, against about 50% if the agent had no effect. When they had already stated their own preference, they switched 60% of the time if the agent disagreed, compared with 6% if it agreed. Afterward, 77% explained their choices with the agent&#039;s arguments, sometimes word for word, but only 15% credited the agent.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> If you use an agent for analysis, it&#039;s probably making more of your judgment calls than you think, even when it argues honestly. The fix is process: decide the key choices before you ask the agent.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Analysts, data scientists, researchers and anyone who uses Claude Code or another agent to make decisions with more than one defensible answer.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">The research post and full paper are free to read on Anthropic&#039;s site. The study used Claude Code; the lesson applies to any AI agent.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/agents-judgment-calls" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Anthropic on www.anthropic.com ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/claude/analysts-followed-coin-flip-86-percent/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
