        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/github-copilot-gpt-6-1-terra/" class="hover:text-brand-600">GPT-6.1 Terra just hit GitHub Copilot everywhere, from VS Code to Xcode, at a lower cost than Sol</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">GitHub made OpenAI&#039;s GPT-6.1 Terra generally available in GitHub Copilot. GitHub pitches it for everyday agentic coding and terminal work at a lower cost than GPT-6.1 Sol, and says in early testing it solved noticeably more terminal and C# tasks than GPT-5.6 Terra. You pick it from the model picker in VS Code, Visual Studio, Copilot CLI, the Copilot cloud agent, the Copilot app, github.com, GitHub Mobile, JetBrains IDEs, Xcode and Eclipse.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> If you already pay for Copilot, you can try OpenAI&#039;s newest cost-efficient model in the editor you use today, without a separate OpenAI account.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">GitHub Copilot users on paid plans, and admins deciding which models their teams can use.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Generally available from October 26, 2026 on Copilot Pro, Pro+, Max, Business and Enterprise, rolling out gradually. It&#039;s billed at the provider&#039;s list price under usage-based billing. Business and Enterprise admins turn it on or off through the Copilot model policy.</dd></div>        </dl>
