        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/" class="rounded-full px-2.5 py-1 font-semibold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/15 dark:text-emerald-300">ChatGPT</a>        <span class="rounded-full bg-zinc-100 px-2.5 py-1 font-medium text-slate-600 dark:bg-white/5 dark:text-zinc-300">Productivity</span>
        <time datetime="2026-10-15" class="text-slate-400">Oct 15, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/chatgpt/browser-extension-tabs/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Let ChatGPT read your open tabs, or act in them, from the browser extension</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">The ChatGPT browser extension now opens a side chat beside the page you are on. It can use your open tabs, text you highlight and YouTube transcripts as context. In a Work or Codex chat on the desktop app you can @-mention Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera or Vivaldi to have ChatGPT carry out the task in that browser, on sites where you are already signed in. By default it asks before it uses each new website.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> ChatGPT can work with the pages you already have open and sites you&#039;re signed in to, so you stop copy-pasting between tabs.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Researchers, shoppers and anyone who lives in their browser.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">ChatGPT browser extension for Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera and Vivaldi; side chat isn&#039;t in Opera.</dd></div>        </dl>
