        <a href="/trending/muse/wonder-vs-opus-game-test/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Muse Wonder vs Opus 5.5: a one-prompt 3D game test</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">On launch day, a developer gave Muse Code (Muse Wonder, extra-high effort) and Claude Code (Opus 5.5, extra-high effort) the same one-shot job: a playable first-person 3D game of his kitchen in a single three.js file. He reports both ran first try; Opus took 11m40s and about $2.10, Wonder took 3m42s and about $1.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Side-by-side one-prompt tests are a cheap way to compare speed and cost on the kind of work you actually do.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Developers deciding whether to try Muse Code or Muse Wonder.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Muse Code with Muse Wonder through the API; Wonder Full needs a larger Muse plan.</dd></div>        </dl>
