        <a href="/trending/muse/revenue-feedback-loop/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Use Muse to explain what moved revenue this week</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">A marketer described a weekly routine: connect Muse to the tools that each see part of the business (Shopify for sales, Stripe for payments, QuickBooks for spending, Meta Ads for demand, Klaviyo for email, Slack and Notion for plans), then give it business questions instead of tool-by-tool ones. His starter prompt asks Muse to explain what moved revenue this week, show the evidence, and turn it into the next campaign brief.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Answers that need data from five tools usually take an afternoon of exports. One connected agent can trace them in one go.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Ecommerce founders and small teams with their tools spread across several apps.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Muse with the relevant connectors; which connectors are available depends on your plan and region.</dd></div>        </dl>
