        <time datetime="2026-10-02" class="text-slate-400">Oct 2, 2026</time>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-ink dark:text-white">
        <a href="/trending/muse/gadgets-diy/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Build your own Muse gadget with Meta&#039;s open-source SDKs</a>    </h3>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Muse Gadgets is Meta&#039;s open-source project for building your own hardware that talks to Muse. You can program an off-the-shelf ESP32 board or turn a Raspberry Pi or other Linux box into a gadget, then connect Muse to screens, buttons, sensors and actuators. The SDKs and firmware are Apache 2.0. TechCrunch reports project ideas such as a colour e-ink display and an HDMI stick for a TV.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-zinc-200">Why it matters:</strong> Muse can now live outside your phone on hardware you design, which is a big deal for makers and home-automation fans.</p>
                    <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Good for:</dt> <dd class="inline">Makers, Raspberry Pi and ESP32 hobbyists, and Home Assistant users.</dd></div>            <div><dt class="inline font-medium text-slate-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Plan &amp; availability:</dt> <dd class="inline">Free and open source; you need a Muse account and an SDK token to pair a gadget.</dd></div>        </dl>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 pt-5 text-sm">
        <a href="https://gadgets.muse.ai/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-brand-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Source: @Meta on gadgets.muse.ai ↗</a>
        <a href="/trending/muse/gadgets-diy/" class="font-semibold text-brand-600">How to try it →</a>    </div>
